    <p class="chapter-number">Chapter One</p>
    <h1>The Storm Window</h1>
  </header>

  <p>The wind had teeth at twenty-three thousand feet.</p>

    <p>It came screaming off the Annapurna massif without warning, as it
    always did, hurling curtains of loose snow sideways across the ridge
    like the exhalation of some indifferent god. Below, the village of
    Thonak &mdash; a scatter of whitewashed stone houses clinging to the
    mountain's flank like barnacles on the hull of a vast, ancient
    ship &mdash; disappeared entirely behind the whiteout. Above, there was
    nothing but gray sky and the suggestion of a summit that had swallowed
    whole expeditions and forgotten them.</p>

    <p>And in between, moving at a speed that no living thing had any right
    to move at this altitude, was GoldenPaw.</p>

    <figure>
      <img src="https://pfst.cf2.poecdn.net/base/image/d56a15a61d28172546b6051b5277dbcfdd058be2e5dbfbb24b293ec17ce4fb6a?w=1376&amp;h=768&amp;pmaid=653164405"
           alt="GoldenPaw sprinting along a snowy mountain ridgeline, golden cape streaming behind him against a gray storm sky.">
      <figcaption>GoldenPaw hits the ridgeline at full stride, a muscular
      blur against the gray storm.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p>He hit the ridgeline at full stride, four massive paws finding
    purchase on wind-scoured granite with an ease that would have baffled
    any mountaineer who happened to witness it &mdash; not that any could
    have kept their eyes focused on something moving that fast. His body
    was a long, muscular streak against the white and gray of the mountain,
    the distinctive amber and black rosette pattern of his coat vivid even
    in the flat, diffuse light of the storm. The golden bracers on his
    wrists caught what little sun filtered through the overcast and threw
    it back as warm, honeyed light. His amber eyes &mdash; wide-set, sharp
    as the mountain's own ridges &mdash; scanned the terrain below with the
    absolute, unhurried focus that was his most dangerous quality.</p>

    <p>Not speed. Not strength. Focus.</p>

    <figure>
      <img src="https://pfst.cf2.poecdn.net/base/image/87b41a0d6fd0be657ee5797e60765558727d73a01f6255640db94ca8af27028e?w=1376&amp;h=768&amp;pmaid=653164410"
           alt="Close-up of GoldenPaw's face, amber eyes gazing intently past the camera, golden cape and engraved bracer visible against storm clouds.">
      <figcaption>Those amber eyes &mdash; not speed, not strength, but
      absolute, unhurried focus.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p>He dropped from the ridge onto a near-vertical ice face and simply
    ran down it, claws extending instinctively with each footfall, punching
    through the surface crust just enough for grip before pulling free in
    the next stride. The wind screamed. He leaned into it.</p>

    <figure>
      <img src="https://pfst.cf2.poecdn.net/base/image/f0cfeb3d148047961c459f8a2b54ceb57ee1951b6a9c69544a1b8d6950f93ca7?w=1376&amp;h=768&amp;pmaid=653164409"
           alt="GoldenPaw charging headlong down a steep glacier face, ice fragments scattering, cape whipping upward behind him.">
      <figcaption>He dropped onto the near-vertical ice face and simply ran
      down it, claws punching through the crust with each stride.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p><em>Dawa.</em> The name had been in his head since Kathmandu, since
    the frantic call from the mountain rescue coordination office.
    <em>Twelve years old. Separated from a school group on the Annapurna
    Circuit above Thorong La. Storm window closing in forty minutes. Maybe
    less.</em></p>

    <p>Forty minutes had been twenty minutes ago.</p>

    <p>He cleared a buttress of black rock in a single, arcing leap that
    carried him forty feet through the howling air, landed in a deep drift
    that would have swallowed a person to the waist, and was through it and
    moving again before the displaced snow had finished settling. His nose
    was already working &mdash; cutting through the cold mineral bite of
    the altitude and the clean, nothing smell of fresh snow &mdash;
    searching for the warm, frightened, living scent of a child.</p>

    <figure>
      <img src="https://pfst.cf2.poecdn.net/base/image/f7773a27e578d99f858e7dedee787f0becbd36dbf122b6f5a354aafa09e1f769?w=1376&amp;h=768&amp;pmaid=653164412"
           alt="GoldenPaw in mid-leap high above a chasm between snowy peaks, body fully extended, golden cape streaming like a comet's tail.">
      <figcaption>A single arcing leap carried him forty feet through the
      howling air, golden cape streaming like a comet's tail.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p>The Himalayas did not care about children. They did not care about
    anything. That was the first thing GoldenPaw had understood when he had
    come here, three years ago, following a thread of instinct and old
    grief up from the sun-baked plains and into these impossible,
    indifferent heights. The mountains were not cruel. They were simply
    complete. They required nothing and offered nothing, and everything
    that lived in their shadow did so entirely on its own terms.</p>

    <figure>
      <img src="https://pfst.cf2.poecdn.net/base/image/7e99d982e1549228a7f4a97cc96474f0ca45a28d1d11a6b498191734be859ec4?w=1376&amp;h=768&amp;pmaid=653164406"
           alt="GoldenPaw crouched on a high rock outcrop in a weathered golden cloak, gazing out over a vast mist-filled mountain valley.">
      <figcaption>Three years ago he had followed grief up from the plains
      and into these impossible, indifferent heights &mdash; and made his
      peace with them.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p>He had made his peace with that.</p>

    <p>A shift in the wind. A thermal rolling up from the valley, carrying
    with it the ghost of a charcoal fire, yak butter, wool &mdash; and
    beneath all of that, faint and ragged and terrified, the scent of a
    small human being.</p>

    <figure>
      <img src="https://pfst.cf2.poecdn.net/base/image/dd98acce4e8ca64da0479c9967f4dff705fc736912d496bceaa3221308fff843?w=1376&amp;h=768&amp;pmaid=653164408"
           alt="GoldenPaw racing across a windswept snow slope, cape streaming, paw prints trailing behind him, a warm glow on the wind ahead.">
      <figcaption>A thermal from the valley carried the faint, ragged scent
      of a child; GoldenPaw changed direction without breaking
      stride.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p>GoldenPaw changed direction without breaking stride, forty degrees
    to the east, pushing harder now, the muscles in his haunches firing in
    long, explosive waves. His breath came in rhythmic, visible clouds. The
    storm tightened around him like a fist.</p>

    <figure>
      <img src="https://pfst.cf2.poecdn.net/base/image/83b3f4722f381e8a9285c690b11ba4f40b9fb90c33a5dba1291482a5b7f476bb?w=1376&amp;h=768&amp;pmaid=653164411"
           alt="GoldenPaw plunging through deep powder at the base of a dark cliff, snow exploding around him, teeth bared with effort.">
      <figcaption>The muscles in his haunches fired in long, explosive
      waves as the storm tightened around him like a fist.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p><em>Hold on, Dawa,</em> he thought, though there was no one to hear
    it. <em>Just hold on.</em></p>

    <figure>
      <img src="https://pfst.cf2.poecdn.net/base/image/ee21597669b38ffdfc90f4a34e69a88d3fa5c5a278e7ec052c22ba73e888f969?w=1376&amp;h=768&amp;pmaid=653164407"
           alt="GoldenPaw kneeling in the snow beneath a granite overhang, one paw resting on the shoulder of a boy in a red jacket, firelight glowing behind them.">
      <figcaption>Sheltered beneath a granite overhang, a small fire
      burning between them, GoldenPaw had found Dawa.</figcaption>
    </figure>

